## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical DP reasoning: variable step costs require deriving both a memoized and a tabular solution, handle unreachable states, and discuss trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Top-down DP with memoization
- Bottom-up DP (tabulation)
- Handling unreachable states with Infinity
- Time/space complexity: O(n|S|) time, O(n) space

## Code Example

```javascript
function minCostTopDown(n, steps, memo=new Map()){
  if(n===0) return 0
  if(n<0) return Infinity
  if(memo.has(n)) return memo.get(n)
  let best = Infinity
  for(let s of steps){
    const c = s + minCostTopDown(n-s, steps, memo)
    if(c < best) best = c
  }
  memo.set(n, best)
  return best
}

function minCostBottomUp(n, steps){
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(INF)
  dp[0] = 0
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let s of steps){
      if(i>=s && dp[i-s] !== INF){
        dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], s + dp[i-s])
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === INF ? -1 : dp[n]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if costs were a non-linear function cost(s) instead of s? 
- How would you extend to count the number of ways to reach n with min cost, not just the cost itself?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates mastery of several advanced graph techniques: DAG dynamic programming, multi-criteria optimization, path reconstruction, and cycle handling. It tests the ability to reason about edge attributes with different behaviors (skip-able vs mandatory latency) and implement lexicographic optimization where hops are prioritized over latency.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sorting for efficient DAG processing
- Lexicographic multi-objective optimization (hops first, latency second)
- Predecessor-based path reconstruction
- Cycle detection using DFS and graph condensation
- Edge attribute handling with conditional weight computation

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(graph, s, t) {
  // Assumes graph is a DAG. Each edge has: latency w, skip boolean
  const order = topologicalSort(graph);
  const best = new Map();
  const pred = new Map();

  best.set(s, [0, 0]); // [hops, latency]

  for (const u of order) {
    if (!best.has(u)) continue;
    const [hops, latency] = best.get(u);

    for (const {to: v, latency: w, skip} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      const cand = [hops + 1, latency + (skip ? 0 : w)];
      const current = best.get(v);
      if (!current || lexicographicallySmaller(cand, current)) {
        best.set(v, cand);
        pred.set(v, u);
      }
    }
  }

  return reconstructPath(pred, s, t);
}

function detectCycles(graph) {
  const visited = new Set();
  const recStack = new Set();

  function dfs(u) {
    if (recStack.has(u)) return true; // Cycle detected
    if (visited.has(u)) return false;

    visited.add(u);
    recStack.add(u);

    for (const {to: v} of graph.edgesFrom(u)) {
      if (dfs(v)) return true;
    }

    recStack.delete(u);
    return false;
  }

  return Array.from(graph.nodes()).some(dfs);
}
```

## Adaptation for Cyclic Graphs
If cycles are present, first detect them using DFS with a recursion stack. For graphs with cycles, apply strongly connected components (SCC) decomposition to create a condensation DAG, then solve on the condensed graph. Alternatively, use Dijkstra's algorithm modified for lexicographic optimization where the edge weight is the tuple (1, w or 0 based on skip).

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply DFS for topological ordering and then DP-style relaxation on a DAG to compute shortest paths without a heap. Also checks path reconstruction via a parent array.

## Key Concepts

- DFS-based topological sort
- DAG single-source shortest path via relaxation in topo order
- Path reconstruction with a parent array
- Complexity: O(n+m)

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(n, edges, start, target) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) g[u].push([v, w]);
  const visited = Array(n).fill(false);
  const topo = [];
  function dfs(u){
    visited[u] = true;
    for (const [v] of g[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
    topo.push(u);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
  topo.reverse();
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u] === INF) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of g[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        parent[v] = u;
      }
    }
  }
  if (dist[target] === INF) return {time: -1, path: []};
  const path = [];
  for (let x = target; x != -1; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);
  path.reverse();
  return {time: dist[target], path};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle multiple sources or tie-breaking paths?
- How would you adapt if the graph could contain cycles (cycle detection and fallback to Dijkstra)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP design for constrained substring deletions, palindrome preprocessing, and reconstruction of selections. Combines memoized and tabulated forms, plus memory/time tradeoffs on a practical string problem.

## Key Concepts

- Palindrome preprocessing (center expansion / DP)
- DP over prefixes with up to k deletions
- Reconstructing solution under tie scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxReward(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const P = Array.from({length:n}, ()=>Array(n).fill(false));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ P[i][i]=true; for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){ /* fill later */ } }
  // assume proper palindrome fill here
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  for(let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[0][t]=0;
  // compute bottom-up as described
  return dp[n][0];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of a practical data structure for real-world features (auto-complete) and how to extend it with per-word frequencies.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with terminal frequency tracking
- DFS/BFS to collect candidate words from a prefix
- Sorting by frequency then lexicographic order
- Edge cases: missing prefix, zero matches, tie handling

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.freq = 0; // frequency for word ending here
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    node.freq += 1;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    const dfs = (n, path) => {
      if (n.freq > 0) results.push([path, n.freq]);
      for (const [ch, child] of n.next) dfs(child, path + ch);
    };
    dfs(node, prefix);
    results.sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return results.slice(0, K).map(v => v[0]);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prune rarely-used branches to save memory?
- How would you support concurrent inserts and autocomplete in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about multi-key sorting, stability, and practical trade-offs in beginner-friendly tasks. It also aligns with real-world data processing where groupings matter.

## Key Concepts

- Sorting stability and its impact on multi-key ordering
- Two-pass stable sorting technique
- Time complexity: O(n log n) for mergesort
- Space complexity: O(n) auxiliary memory

## Code Example

```javascript
function stableMergeSortBy(arr, key) {
  if (arr.length <= 1) return arr
  const mid = Math.floor(arr.length / 2)
  const left = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(0, mid), key)
  const right = stableMergeSortBy(arr.slice(mid), key)
  const out = []
  let i = 0, j = 0
  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i][key] <= right[j][key]) out.push(left[i++])
    else out.push(right[j++])
  }
  return out.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j))
}

function sortRecords(arr) {
  const byTime = stableMergeSortBy(arr, 'time')
  return stableMergeSortBy(byTime, 'priority')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify if you must sort in place and the library sort is not stable?
- If time values span a large range, how could you optimize space or use counting/radix techniques to reduce complexity?

## Why This Is Asked
Design a structure to maintain the k-th largest element efficiently in a streaming context, using a heap and a fixed-size container.

## Key Concepts
- Min-heap for thresholding top-k
- Time: O(log k) per insert
- Edge cases: fewer than k elements, duplicates

## Code Example
```javascript
class KthLargest {
  constructor(k, nums = []) {
    this.k = k; this.heap = [];
    for (const n of nums) this.add(n);
  }
  add(val) {
    if (this.heap.length < this.k) { this.heap.push(val); this._bubbleUp(this.heap.length-1); }
    else if (val > this.heap[0]) { this.heap[0] = val; this._siftDown(0); }
    return this.heap.length === this.k ? this.heap[0] : null;
  }
  // ... helpers omitted for brevity
}
```

